------------------------------------------------------
SLIDE 4 - A SKILL IS JUST A FOLDER
------------------------------------------------------
A skill is just a folder with a Markdown file in it.

(pause)

That surprises people. It's not a model, it's not a
plugin, it's not a service. It's a text file called
SKILL.md.

At the top there are two required fields. The name. And
the description. (point) The description is the most
important line in the whole file. The agent reads it to
decide whether to use the skill, so it says what the
skill does and when to use it.

Below that is the procedure itself, written in plain
English. And optionally, the folder can hold scripts the
agent can run and reference docs it can read.

This example is a real skill from my library. It does
read-only Kubernetes triage. You give it the output of a
failing pod, and it tells you what's wrong and the next
command to run. It never touches the cluster itself.

Because it's just files, it lives in git. It gets
reviewed in pull requests and has history, like any
other code.

(pause) So what happens when an agent has a lot of these?

------------------------------------------------------
SLIDE 5 - PROGRESSIVE DISCLOSURE
------------------------------------------------------
An agent only reads what the task needs.

Think of it like the table of contents in a book. When
the agent starts up, it reads only the chapter titles:
the name and description of each skill. That's about a
hundred tokens per skill, which is tiny.

When a task matches one of those descriptions, it opens
that one chapter and reads the full instructions.

And only if a specific step needs it does it pull in a
script or a reference document.

What that means for us is that we can give an agent
hundreds of skills without slowing it down or running up
the bill. It only pays for what it actually uses.

(pause) And these skills aren't tied to one product.

------------------------------------------------------
SLIDE 6 - OPEN STANDARD
------------------------------------------------------
Agent Skills is an open standard. Anthropic created the
format and released it openly, and now a long list of
tools read it: GitHub Copilot, VS Code, Cursor, Gemini
CLI, OpenAI's Codex, and plenty more.

For us, the point is simple. We write a skill once and
use it anywhere. If we change tools next year, our
skills come with us. We're not locked into a vendor.

The spec lives at agentskills.io, and I'll pull it up
during the demo.

(pause) So skills tell an agent how to do something. But
for me, something was still missing.

------------------------------------------------------
SLIDE 7 - PERSONA
------------------------------------------------------
Skills say how. A persona says who.

(pause)

A persona is basically a role description, the same
kind you'd write for a new hire. It says: here's your
role, here are the standards you work to, and here's
what you never do.

(point at Hard limits) This last one is the one that
matters most. My DevOps persona has no cloud
credentials, and it never applies infrastructure. It
writes the code and the pipeline, and a person approves.

Here's why personas matter. I have a junior DevOps kit
and a senior DevOps kit. They share the exact same
skills. The difference is the persona and the model. The
junior runs on a cheaper model and handles well-scoped
work, like lint fixes and version bumps. When it hits
anything involving permissions, networking or
infrastructure state, it stops and flags the pull
request for senior review.

Same procedures. Different judgment.

(pause) So how do I put a persona and its skills
together? That's a kit.

------------------------------------------------------
SLIDE 8 - KIT
------------------------------------------------------
A kit bundles everything for one role into a single JSON
file.

(point at the right column) It says who the agent is,
which skills it can pick up, which team rules it reads
every session, the only tools it's allowed to use, and
which model it runs on.

The easiest way to think about it: a kit is the job
description plus the toolbox for one hire.

And because it's one file, if I change the kit, every
agent I start after that gets the change.

