SLIDE 10 - FROM KIT TO PULL REQUEST
------------------------------------------------------
Every agent goes from a kit to a pull request.

I pick a kit. The agent gets its own branch, or its own
container, so it can't step on anyone else's work. The
persona, skills and team rules get copied in, and it's
limited to the tools the kit names. Then it does the
work, pulling in skills as it needs them. And it ends
with commits on its own branch that a person reviews and
merges.

Two design choices worth calling out. (point) I can run
several agents at once, each in its own tab. And none of
them ever holds a real credential. The agent gets a
stand-in token, and the real credentials stay on my
machine.

(pause) Which leads to the question everyone asks: can
we trust it?

------------------------------------------------------
SLIDE 11 - GUARDRAILS
------------------------------------------------------
Skills can run commands, so we treat them like code.

The IBM video is upfront about the risks, things like
prompt injection and tool poisoning. So the whole design
assumes the agent can be wrong.

(point at two cards) The two that matter most: it never
holds real credentials, and a person always merges.
Nothing it does reaches a live environment without
review.

The rest is here for you to read: least tools, skills
reviewed in PRs, kits scanned for secrets, and guardrail
hooks with an audit trail.

(pause) So what does this mean for us as an
organization?

------------------------------------------------------
SLIDE 12 - WHAT THE ORGANIZATION GETS
------------------------------------------------------
Four things.

Consistency. The way our best engineer does a task
becomes the default, instead of depending on who's on
shift.

Auditability. The procedures live in git, and
everything an agent does arrives as commits a person
reviews. That matters for CMMC.

Portability. It's an open standard, so we're not betting
on one vendor.

And cost. Each kit picks its model, so routine work runs
on cheaper models and only the hard problems use the
strongest one.

